qtbase: make the primary QConcatenable template a complete (empty) type

QStringBuilder declares a global `operator%` that is meant to be removed
from overload resolution for non-concatenable types via the SFINAE guard
`std::void_t<typename QConcatenableEx<A>::type, ...>`. Because the primary
`QConcatenable` template is only forward-declared, accessing `::type` for a
foreign type triggers "implicit instantiation of undefined template" which
is a hard error under clang rather than a substitution failure.

This surfaces on macOS (clang) when Boost.Date_Time's internal modulo on
`boost::gregorian::greg_year` is resolved while Qt's `operator%` is in scope,
breaking the build of essentially every Qt translation unit that transitively
includes Boost (via boost/thread -> boost/date_time).

Giving the primary template an empty definition turns the `::type` access
into a clean substitution failure, so `operator%` is correctly discarded and
the built-in/Boost operator is used. Specializations are unaffected, so valid
QStringBuilder concatenations keep working.

--- a/qtbase/src/corelib/text/qstringbuilder.h
+++ b/qtbase/src/corelib/text/qstringbuilder.h
@@ -31,7 +31,7 @@ struct Q_CORE_EXPORT QAbstractConcatenable
     static void appendLatin1To(QLatin1StringView in, QChar *out) noexcept;
 };
 
-template <typename T> struct QConcatenable;
+template <typename T> struct QConcatenable {};
 
 template <typename T>
 using QConcatenableEx = QConcatenable<q20::remove_cvref_t<T>>;
